Now I need to understand the vulnerability. The description says "Global-buffer-overflow READ 1" in the `fuzzer_tacacs` target. Let me look at the `tacacs_decode_args` function more carefully. 

In `tacacs_decode_args`:
```c
uint8_t buffer[256];
...
if (arg_list[i] < 2) goto next;
...
memcpy(buffer, p, arg_list[i]);
buffer[arg_list[i]] = '\0';
```

`arg_list[i]` is a `uint8_t`, so it can be up to 255. `buffer` is 256 bytes. `memcpy(buffer, p, 255)` copies 255 bytes, then `buffer[255] = '\0'` writes at index 255, which is valid. So that's fine.

But wait - let me look at the `raw:` label more carefully:

```c
da = fr_dict_attr_by_name(NULL, fr_dict_root(dict_tacacs), (char *) buffer);
if (!da) {
raw:
        /*
         *      Dupe the whole thing so that we have:
         *
         *      Argument-List += "name=value"
         */
        da = parent;
        value = p;
        arg_end = p + arg_list[i];
}
```

When `da` is not found, it goes to `raw:` and sets `value = p` and `arg_end = p + arg_list[i]`. Then later:

```c
if (fr_pair_value_from_str(vp, (char const *) value, arg_end - value, 0, true) < 0) {
        talloc_free(vp);
        if (da != parent) goto raw;
        goto next;
}
```

If `fr_pair_value_from_str` fails and `da != parent`, it goes to `raw:` again. But at `raw:`, `da = parent` is set, so the second time it won't go to raw again. That seems fine.

